 For tasks configured for semantic recovery, LogicFly distinguishes three levels of negative evidence. A \textbf{pose-level miss} removes the attempted candidate while keeping the task active. \textbf{Region-level exhaustion} invokes alternative room--functional-region hypotheses grounded in the hierarchy; these hypotheses become geometric observation regions and are ranked using semantic relevance, coverage, and travel cost. A \textbf{task-level negative outcome} is produced when the task-specific stopping condition is reached, after which CPTG updates downstream logic. Within semantic recovery, newly covered portions of an unproductive region reduce its belief, shifting search toward alternative hypotheses.
+
+For a semantic recovery region $r$, let $\mathcal P_r$ be its fixed sample set and $\mathcal O_{r,n}$ the samples already covered before verification $n$, initialized as $\mathcal O_{r,0}=\varnothing$. With visible samples $\mathcal V_r(\mathbf q_n)$, define
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{aligned}
+\delta_{r,n}&=\frac{|\mathcal V_r(\mathbf q_n)\setminus\mathcal O_{r,n}|}
+{\max(1,|\mathcal P_r|)},\\
+\mathcal O_{r,n+1}&=\mathcal O_{r,n}\cup\mathcal V_r(\mathbf q_n).
+\end{aligned}
+\label{eq:recovery-coverage}
+\end{equation}
+The task-local confidence $b_{r,0}$ is initialized from semantic relevance: high, medium, low, and fallback hypotheses receive $1.0$, $0.6$, $0.3$, and $0.15$. Registration preserves an existing region's accumulated state. If $y_n\in\{0,1\}$ indicates successful target verification and $Q_r(\mathbf q_n)$ is geometric view quality, the update is
+\begin{equation}
+b_{r,n+1}=
+\begin{cases}
+1,&y_n=1,\\
+b_{r,n}\bigl[1-\alpha\delta_{r,n}Q_r(\mathbf q_n)\bigr],&y_n=0,
